Steady-state compositional simulation of gas--liquid flow in wells, flowlines and risers couples a cubic equation of state and an isothermal flash to mixture momentum and energy balances closed by homogeneous or flow-pattern-dependent mechanistic relations. This article, the first of two, presents a simulator in which the physics is written once, in flux form, and executed on an array backend with automatic differentiation (JAX): the Jacobian of the conserved fluxes, the derivatives of the flash with respect to pressure and temperature and the sparse Newton Jacobians are exact, and the compiled program is vectorised over cases. A no-slip and a mechanistic model are solved by an adaptive Runge--Kutta march and by finite-difference and finite-volume nodal systems; the three methods agree to 0.01 bar and 0.1 K, the finite volumes close the momentum and energy balances to machine precision, and Newton converges in one to three iterations across phase and flow-pattern changes. The thermodynamic kernel reproduces an independent open-source Peng--Robinson implementation to a relative 1e-6. Compared with Aspen HYSYS, the phase envelope of an 11-component fluid agrees to 0.7 bar in the median; in 100 vertical lines the mechanistic model falls inside the range of four HYSYS correlations in 88 % of the cases, with a median deviation of 1 % from their mean, while in 100 short horizontal lines with a heavy pseudo-component both models lie below that range, which itself spans a factor of three, by a factor of about four concentrated in stratified flow that neither the wall roughness nor the liquid viscosity closes and that equals the ratio of the Darcy to the Fanning friction factor; along an offshore well--flowline--riser system the mechanistic pressure follows the Beggs and Brill profile of HYSYS within about 1 bar after the well and the temperatures agree within 2 K. Once compiled, the nodal solvers run the offshore system in about 0.6 s.
